<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n46611" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sŭpĕrābĭlis</span></b> ,
		e, adj.
		supero, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>that may be got over</i> or <i>surmounted.</i> * </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">murus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Liv. 25, 23, 12</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>that may be
			overcome</i> or <i>subdued</i>, <i>conquerable</i>, <i>superable</i> (very rare): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">non est per vim superabilis ulli,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					Tr. 5, 8, 27</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">invictos et nullis casibus superabiles Romanos
					praedicabant,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tac. A.
					2, 25</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">an talis caecitas ac debilitas ope
					humanā superabiles forent,</span>”
				i. e. <i>curable</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">id. H.
					4, 81</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
